# `owned <freer>` on a `@` cell, made to RUN (design.md §4.19; panel 109's
# conditions 4 and 8, ratified 2026-09-04).
#
# **The direction the milestone finished with.** A `cstr` a C function RETURNS
# and marks `owned` has been freed since M-declared-freer step 4; this is the
# other position, where C writes a pointer into a cell the caller supplies and
# the caller owns what lands there. `sqlite3_exec`'s `char **errmsg` is the
# shape, `examples/ledger/` is the program, and this case is the part of it
# that runs on a machine with no SQLite.
#
# Three facts the spec's FFI clause states, one call each:
#   1. the cell reaches the program as a `str?` and never as the `cstr` the
#      header spells — by then the bytes are copied and the original freed;
#   2. the same call works twice, so nothing is left holding a stale pointer;
#   3. a C function that never writes the cell hands back `null_cstr` rather
#      than freeing whatever the stack held.
#
# **The third is the one no library will show you.** SQLite writes a null on
# success, so the `null_cstr` branch has no witness in the corpus at all —
# `hero_owned_silent` in the header beside this file exists to be the shape a
# polite library never is.
#
# **AND THIS CASE CANNOT SEE THE NULL STORE MISSING, WHICH WAS MEASURED RATHER
# THAN ARGUED.** This comment first claimed the opposite. With the lowering's
# `store $cell0 <- const nullptr` deleted and the compiler rebuilt, the four
# lines below are printed UNCHANGED, exit 0, at `-O0` and under
# `-fsanitize=address,undefined`: the C local is declared and never assigned,
# reading it is undefined behaviour, and on this machine it happened to hold
# zero. Measured across three suites on 2026-09-07 — `run` **0 failed**,
# `ir` **1** (`ir/owned-cell`), `emission` **3**. So the instruments that can
# see it are the STRUCTURAL ones, and that is panel 116's finding about the
# NULL guard arriving one level over: a guard no local run can falsify can
# never be justified by a green local run either.
#
# **And what nothing HERE can see is the leak**: LeakSanitizer does not exist
# on Darwin arm64 (CLAUDE.md §7), so this program is green on the author's Mac
# whether or not `free` is ever called. The Linux leg under `--sanitize` is
# the judge, which is CLAUDE.md §8's rule about a program that declares an
# `extern`, and this file is one of the eight it names.
extern "stdlib.h"
    function free(p: ptr)

extern "ffi-owned-cell-is-freed.h"
    function hero_owned_describe(code: i32, @out: cstr owned free) -> i32
    function hero_owned_silent(code: i32, @out: cstr owned free) -> i32

function main()
    first: str? @ fail(code: "none", msg: "nothing yet")
    _ = hero_owned_describe(code: 0, out: @first)
    print(first.default("<nothing>"))

    # Twice, into a second cell: the copy is the program's own, so nothing
    # here is looking at memory the compiler has already released.
    second: str? @ fail(code: "none", msg: "nothing yet")
    _ = hero_owned_describe(code: 7, out: @second)
    print(second.default("<nothing>"))

    # `false` is the point: two calls, two independent strings.
    print(first.default("") == second.default(""))

    # The cell C never wrote. `.err` and not a crash, and the code says which
    # of the two failures it is.
    quiet: str? @ fail(code: "none", msg: "nothing yet")
    _ = hero_owned_silent(code: 3, out: @quiet)

    match quiet
        .ok text => print("wrote: ", text)
        .err e   => print("nothing written: ", e.code)
